{"description":"<p>The best-selling novel <i>Black Beauty</i> by Anna Sewell was published in 1877. The story is a first person narrative told from the perspective of the horse Black Beauty. This unique narrative perspective enables readers to empathize with the lives of working horses and to reflect upon the cruel treatment that has been inflicted upon them. As a result, the novel serves as a strenuous and timeless statement against animal cruelty and exploitation.</p>","chapters":[{"title":"I: My Early Home","part":"Part I","words":780},{"title":"II: The Hunt","part":"Part I","words":1019},{"title":"III: My Breaking In","part":"Part I","words":1449},{"title":"IV: Birtwick Park","part":"Part I","words":983},{"title":"V: A Fair Start","part":"Part I","words":1247},{"title":"VI: Liberty","part":"Part I","words":561},{"title":"VII: Ginger","part":"Part I","words":1593},{"title":"VIII: Ginger’s Story Continued","part":"Part I","words":1305},{"title":"IX: Merrylegs","part":"Part I","words":996},{"title":"X: A Talk in the Orchard","part":"Part I","words":1941},{"title":"XI: Plain Speaking","part":"Part I","words":1056},{"title":"XII: A Stormy Day","part":"Part I","words":1154},{"title":"XIII: The Devil’s Trade Mark","part":"Part I","words":1003},{"title":"XIV: James Howard","part":"Part I","words":964},{"title":"XV: The Old Hostler","part":"Part I","words":999},{"title":"XVI: The Fire","part":"Part I","words":1336},{"title":"XVII: John Manly’s Talk","part":"Part I","words":1279},{"title":"XVIII: Going for the Doctor","part":"Part I","words":1317},{"title":"XIX: Only Ignorance","part":"Part I","words":759},{"title":"XX: Joe Green","part":"Part I","words":914},{"title":"XXI: The Parting","part":"Part I","words":920},{"title":"XXII: Earlshall","part":"Part II","words":1351},{"title":"XXIII: A Strike for Liberty","part":"Part II","words":1106},{"title":"XXIV: The Lady Anne, or a Runaway Horse","part":"Part II","words":2036},{"title":"XXV: Reuben Smith","part":"Part II","word